[THOUGHT] CRP and troponin were not available in this clinical environment. I must instead request other high-yield, available tests that support or refine the diagnosis of acute pericarditis with effusion. Key alternatives include:  
- Complete blood count (CBC) to assess for leukocytosis (infectious/inflammatory), anemia (chronic disease), or thrombocytosis;  
- Basic metabolic panel (BMP) to evaluate renal function, electrolytes (e.g., hypokalemia exacerbating arrhythmia), and markers of systemic illness;  
- Chest X-ray — though limited for small effusions, a “water-bottle” cardiac silhouette would support large pericardial effusion, and it can also reveal alternative diagnoses (e.g., pneumonia, pulmonary edema, pleural effusion).  
Given the patient’s hypotension and respiratory distress, CXR is rapidly obtainable and provides complementary structural information. It is listed among commonly available tests in ED settings.

[TEST] Chest X-ray (posteroanterior and lateral views).

